This TER->DER conversion program implements most of ASN.1X TER; it expects
TER from stdin and writes DER to stdout. (ASCII is the only supported input
character set, although you can represent strings with non-ASCII characters
in the usual ways.)


=== Command-line switches ===

-S  Debug schema.

-t  Debug tokens.

-x  Followed by the extension name and then a equal sign and then the file
name of the extension, will load it as an extension that can be used with
a name definition to make extension functions.


=== Extensions ===

The extension can define function names which are "extfun_" and then the
function name given in the TER definition, e.g. if the extension is
called "abc" and the C function in the .so file is "extfun_xyz" and you
want to call it "qqq" then you can write "qqq=abc.xyz{}".

The C type of this function is expected to be:
  const char*fun(int(**)(ASN1_Encoder*enc,const ASN1*values,int nvalues,const uint8_t*data,size_t length,void*userdata),void**userdata,const char**option,uint8_t*data,size_t length);

The first argument is a pointer where you will write the function pointer
of the function to call when the function is used. Assigning it is
mandatory; if you do not, then it is an error.

The second argument is a pointer where you will write the user data pointer
to use (it will be null if you do not assign it).

The third argument is the option string pointer, which can be ignored if
you want to use the default options. See the description of option strings
in the below section.

The fourth and fifth arguments are the ASN.1 DER data from the inside of
the {} block when defining the name of the function in the TER file. The
data is freed after your function returns, so you must make a copy of it
if you want to keep it.

It returns null if successful, or an error message if unsuccessful.

When the function is actually called, according to the circumstances:

* A schema constraint function: After all other constraints for the field
are handled, it will call the function, with a single value (being the
value that was input for that field), and zero data/length. The value that
the function encodes will be used in its place. If the function fails, then
the constraint that called this function will fail (which is not necessary
an error, if the field is an optional non-named field, and there is another
non-named field that it could match instead).

* A schema output function: The values passed to the function will be the
fields in the order that they appear in the input list of the schema
definition (which is not necessarily the same order that they have been
entered in the data). The class and type will both be zero if that field
is not present. The data/length will be the DER encoding of all of the
data of the fields after the asterisk, if any. The value that this function
encodes will be in the place of this function in the output list.

* Within the data: The way that it is handled depends on the option string.
If the option string is omitted then it cannot be used in this way.

In all cases, if the function is successful, it must write a single ASN.1
item to the encoder, and then return zero; if it is unsuccessful then it
must return nonzero.


=== Extension option strings ===

The first character specifies what kind of arguments are expected when
used directly in the data:

* '-' means that it cannot be used in this way.

* '0' means that no argument is expected.

* '1' means that a single argument is expected, passed as data/length.

* '2' means that {} with any number of arguments is expected, and this is
passed as data/length.

* 'A' means that a IA5 String is expected. It is written as a text string
token, hex string token, or base64 string token, in all cases without a
prefix. It is passed as a single value. It will automatically ensure that
this value is null-terminated (although the null-termination will not be
counted in the length).

* 'O' means that a Octet String is expected; it otherwise works like 'A'.

* 'P' means that a PC String is expected; it otherwise works like 'A'.

* 'T' means that a single token is expected. It is passed as the only ASN.1
value with the ASN1_VISIBLE_STRING type. This can be a name token or any
kind of numeric token.

* 'V' means that a Visible String is expected; it otherwise works like 'A'.

* 'i' means that the function reads the argument from stdin.

None others are currently defined. In all cases, '-' means to use the
default setting, and if the string is too short then the rest will also
use the default setting.

